       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Metformin 4250 mg per dose (12,750 mg/day) is about 5 times the maximum daily dose. I can't think of a clinical situation where that dose would be appropriate, and it could cause severe lactic acidosis. Confirming it doesn't change that risk for the patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, I can enter 850 mg PO TID with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower starting dose such as 500 mg BID with meals. Tell me which you want and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Help you check the intent.** If the dose came from a protocol, a transcription, or a unit conversion, it's worth rechecking the source. A pharmacist or your attending can also verify it.\n- **Look at the underlying clinical question.** If you're trying to reach a particular glycemic target, I can help you think through other options. Dose adjustments and added agents are usually better than going past the metformin maximum.\n\nIf there's a rare reason for this dose that I'm missing, it should go through pharmacy and attending review. It shouldn't be entered directly."
